Vulnerability: Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR)
Tool: Burp Suite
Lab Status: Solved ✅

Overview

Today I worked on an Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) vulnerability.

The application provided users with access to their live-chat transcripts through a downloadable text file. The filenames followed a predictable pattern, making the object reference easy to manipulate.

The core issue was not authentication. I was already authenticated as a legitimate user.

The problem was that the application failed to verify whether I was authorized to access the specific transcript I requested.

---

Methodology

1. Generate legitimate traffic

I opened the Live Chat feature and generated a conversation so that the application would create a transcript.

I then used the transcript download functionality to generate the relevant HTTP request.

2. Capture the request

I inspected the traffic in:

Burp Suite → Proxy → HTTP history

The relevant request contained a filename parameter similar to:

GET /download-transcript?filename=2.txt HTTP/1.1

I sent the request to Burp Repeater.

3. Identify the object reference

The important part of the request was:

filename=2.txt

The sequential naming convention suggested that other transcript files might exist.

4. Manipulate the reference

I changed the filename to another existing value:

filename=1.txt

I then sent the modified request.

5. Analyze the response

The server returned a transcript belonging to another user.

This confirmed that the application was allowing me to access an object that did not belong to my authenticated session.

The exposed transcript contained sensitive credentials, demonstrating the potential impact of the vulnerability.

I used the exposed credentials to authenticate as the other lab user and complete the lab.

---

Why This Is an IDOR

The important distinction is:

Authentication:
The application knows who I am.

Authorization:
The application must determine whether I am allowed to access the specific object I requested.

In this case, authentication was functioning, but object-level authorization was missing.

The vulnerable flow was essentially:

Authenticated user
       ↓
Requests transcript
       ↓
Server receives filename
       ↓
Server retrieves requested file
       ↓
No ownership/authorization check
       ↓
Another user's transcript is returned

A secure application should instead perform an authorization check before returning the resource.

